   "kernel": "raw CUDA stencil + K-1 sibling reads in one arena"
  },
  "finding": "A raw CUDA 7-point stencil with the identical array shape, identical replica stride (91,260 B), identical wrap topology and identical checkerboard parity shows NO penalty: -0.1%, full overlap, on the same H100 and the same lattice where the JAX kernel gives +12.1%. Adding 1, 2, 4 or 8 same-shaped sibling buffers in one contiguous arena changes nothing (-0.1% to +0.4%). XLA does not pad the batch dimension (argument bytes per replica constant to 0.000%) and uses one layout {4,3,2,1,0} across the window. Conclusion: the penalty is NOT intrinsic to this access pattern on this hardware. It comes from how XLA compiles this particular kernel. Adding the random draws does not change this either. A raw Metropolis substep with three normals and one uniform per site, a uniaxial term, the exp() and the acceptance branch gives -0.2% with the RNG generated in registers and -0.1% with it written to a full-size 4-float-per-site buffer by a separate kernel each substep and read back. Both are flat with full overlap, even though the added work is real: the inline version costs 1.12x the bare stencil and the materialised version 1.33x. So neither the replica stride, nor sibling-buffer arena alignment, nor the generator's traffic explains the +12.1%. What still differs from the compiled kernel: jnp.roll neighbour construction (which XLA may materialise as shifted copies instead of computing modular indices), the per-temperature reductions, and the block and grid dimensions XLA picks per fusion, which derive from an element count that depends on B. The next step needs no new kernel: dump XLA's launch dimensions and fusion configs per B and look for what changes at B = 0 mod 4.",
